DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/FF-DEC-2026\n\nDEC FIRST FRID
 AY | MEMAUD 125 | Open to the Stanford Community | Lunch will be served.\n
 \nThe TAPS Graduate Students host Dr. Vicki L. Hoskins for the December Fi
 rst Friday.\n\nHave you ever given much thought to your theatrical program
  after the usher hands it to you? This talk asks you to consider what role
  a theatrical program plays in shaping Broadway’s cultural identity. Tog
 ether\, we will explore the central argument of Playbill’s Broadway: Mon
 opolies\, Branding\, and Audience Life\, examining how Playbill evolved fr
 om a commercial printing and advertising enterprise into an influential cu
 ltural institution. Along the way\, we’ll reflect on our own relationshi
 ps with playbills\, considering how their familiar presence in the theatre
 -going experience shapes our theatrical expectations. You'll never see a t
 heatrical program the same way.\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER | Dr. Vicki L. Hoskin
 s is an Assistant Professor of Theatre and Dance at San Francisco State Un
 iversity. Her research investigates the economic and cultural structures t
 hat shape the production\, circulation\, and reception of theatre\, with p
 articular attention to Broadway\, musical theatre\, branding\, marketing\,
  and audience culture. Her current book project\, Playbill’s Broadway: M
 onopolies\, Branding\, and Audience Life\, traces the institutional histor
 y of Playbill and examines how the company’s commercial strategies\, ind
 ustry relationships\, and branding practices have shaped Broadway’s cult
 ural identity and audience experiences. Her
